# SRG-APP-000700-WSR-000100  -  CAT II
# The web server must disable accounts when the accounts have expired.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000700-WSR-000100
group:           V-264337
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       manual
confidence:      derived
revised:         yes
cci:             CCI-003627
control_rev5:    AC-2

contribution:    Splunk has no account expiration date for its native accounts - the one
                 expiry mechanism it ships governs passwords, not accounts - so the
                 application's contribution is limited to honoring, at the next
                 authentication, the decision of whatever authority holds the account
                 lifecycle.

external:        The enterprise directory service or identity provider holds the account
                 expiration date and must disable or deprovision the account on expiry;
                 Splunk honors that decision only at the next authentication.
external:        Where accounts are provisioned through SCIM, the provisioning authority
                 sends the disable or delete; the receiving path exists in the product but
                 the decision and the transmission are the authority's.
external:        The organization's account review and offboarding procedure determines
                 when an account has expired.
external:        An assessor confirms the account lifecycle authority and its expiry
                 handling, because the application exposes no expiration value to read.

manual_reason:   Splunk native accounts carry no expiration date field, and nothing in the
                 10.4.2 specification set expresses one, so no configuration value can
                 decide whether expired accounts are disabled.

citation:        All three conditions are invention, and two of them are also vacuous.
                 web-features.conf [feature:identity_sidecar_scim] enabled controls only
                 "whether or not Splunk Web displays Automated User Management (AUM)
                 controls for System for Cross-Domain Identity Management (SCIM) in the
                 SAML configuration dialog page" and defaults to true
                 (web-features.conf.spec:400-410): it is a dialog visibility toggle that
                 provisions nothing and cannot bear on account expiry at all. server.conf
                 [teleport_supervisor] disabled launches the process that "handles
                 authorization tokens for System for Cross-Domain Identity Management
                 (SCIM) operations" and already defaults to false, meaning launched
                 (server.conf.spec:6584-6591), so the condition is unfounded in any source
                 text - this SRG carries no product STIG rule, so both check and fix text
                 are the SRG's own abstract wording - and cannot fail a default
                 installation. authType not_equals Splunk forces delegated authentication
                 the check text never asks for, the same over-reach as in
                 SRG-APP-000315-WSR-000003, and fails an instance whose account lifecycle
                 is managed by an organizational procedure instead. The nearest native
                 setting is expireUserAccounts, and the specification is explicit that it
                 "Specifies whether password expiration is enabled", sitting beside
                 expirePasswordDays and expireAlertDays (authentication.conf.spec:487-507):
                 that is password expiry under the identification and authentication
                 controls, not the account expiration date this requirement's CCI-003627
                 asks about. The two keys the inventory carries for this requirement as
                 undeclared, scimSupportedDomains and scsSyncUserDeletes, appear in no
                 .conf.spec in the 10.4.2 set, so no criterion can be written over them.
finding:         No application-layer finding is available: Splunk native accounts have no
                 expiration date field, so the assessor records the authority that holds
                 the account lifecycle and verifies expiry handling there; raise a finding
                 only where no authority outside the application disables expired accounts.
